# Standing Order: Nielsen Norman UX/UI Compliance

Every interface change in this project (cards, editors, tables, modals, filters,
forms) MUST meet or exceed the Nielsen Norman 10 usability heuristics. Treat this
as a gate on any UI work, not an afterthought.

## The 10 heuristics — how they apply here

1. **Visibility of system status** — Show state without hover. Allocation %, WIP
   status, progress, totals, and validation must be visible on the surface.
2. **Match the real world** — Use PM/engineering language (Responsible, Reviewer,
   Preparer, WBS, EVM), not developer jargon.
3. **User control & freedom** — Every action is undoable (`mutate()`); modals have
   Cancel/Esc; destructive actions confirm.
4. **Consistency & standards** — One source of truth per concept. Never present the
   same data in two competing controls (e.g., a separate "Assignee" dropdown AND a
   resource table — the responsible/lead row IS the assignee).
5. **Error prevention** — Validate before commit. Show live totals (allocation
   should reach 100%), block illegal moves (WIP, dependency gates) with clear text.
6. **Recognition over recall** — Searchable pickers show name + role + department +
   avatar. Never force users to remember codes or type exact strings.
7. **Flexibility & efficiency** — Support keyboard (`/`, `N`, arrows, Enter, Esc),
   search, and sensible defaults; power users and novices both served.
8. **Aesthetic & minimalist design** — Show only what's needed. Add rows on demand;
   don't render empty placeholder rows.
9. **Help users recognize/recover from errors** — Toasts state what failed and how
   to fix it (e.g., "WIP limit reached… finish or pull work out first").
10. **Help & documentation** — Inline hints under complex controls; keep the User
    Guide (`docs/word/`, `docs/USER-GUIDE.md`) in sync with UI changes.

## Concrete pattern in this codebase

```js
// ❌ BAD: hover-only info, two sources of truth, weak native datalist
html += "<span class='avatar' title='Name - 60%'>" + initials + "</span>";
// separate #fAssignee <select> + a 3-row datalist table

// ✅ GOOD: responsible named on the card face with visible %, team as % chips;
// one unified, searchable, validated Team & allocation editor (buildTeamEditor /
// resourcePicker). Lead row = primary assignee. Live allocation total with status.
```

## Enforcement

- Before finishing any UI change, self-review against all 10 heuristics above.
- Prefer the existing `resourcePicker` / `buildTeamEditor` / `cardTeamHTML`
  patterns for any people/allocation UI.
- Add/extend QA checks in `tests/qa.js` for new UI logic; keep the suite green.
- Cite the heuristic(s) addressed in the changelog entry.
